{"kind":"notes","id":"nt/j6verh","slideIndex":1,"text":"Suggested narration (45–60 seconds):\nWe have already built the core product, so this plan focuses on readiness for real use. First, we resolve the verification bypass and test delivery, error handling and safety flows. Next, we add a real recurring-drill scheduler with explicit opt-in, quiet hours, frequency limits and pause controls. A small consenting community cohort will then help us test accessibility and repeated practice. We will compare safe responses across comparable drills, measure repeat participation and operating cost, and use those findings to decide whether to expand.\n\nImplementation detail for questions: Recheck consent and verified destination immediately before every scheduled send, enforce per-user/channel frequency limits and prevent duplicate sends. Provider acceptance does not establish delivery; capture callbacks and show unconnected or ambiguous attempts as unscored. Prompt-based voice safeguards require real testing and should not be described as guaranteed intervention. Treat the pilot partner as prospective. Pilot timing and size are not committed. Behavioural improvement in drills does not itself prove reduced real-world scam losses; use a baseline, comparable scenarios and, where practical, a comparison group. Huawei ECS deployment instructions exist, while ModelArts migration is proposed and needs compatibility, latency and cost validation.\n\nSources:\n/Users/ngwenjing/huawei-2026/docs/superpowers/specs/2026-10-02-drill-mode-roadmap.md\n/Users/ngwenjing/huawei-2026/README.md\n/Users/ngwenjing/huawei-2026/TESTPLAN.md\n/Users/ngwenjing/huawei-2026/DEPLOY.md\nThe stage order and milestone wording are proposed for this presentation, not a claim of committed delivery dates."}